---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { partnerLinks } from '../../lib/data';

const links = partnerLinks(undefined, ['esim']);
---

<Guide slug="esim-travel" photo="tokyo">
  <p>
    An eSIM is a digital SIM card built into your phone. Instead of buying a plastic SIM at the airport, you buy a data plan
    online, scan a QR code or install it from an app, and your phone connects to a local network as soon as you land. For most
    trips it’s far cheaper than your home carrier’s roaming charges.
  </p>

  <h2>How a travel eSIM works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Check your phone supports eSIM</b> and is unlocked (not tied to one carrier).</li>
    <li><b>Buy a plan</b> for your destination or region — for example 5 GB for 15 days in Japan, or a Europe-wide plan.</li>
    <li><b>Install it before you leave</b> while you’re on Wi-Fi. Most plans don’t start counting days until they first connect abroad.</li>
    <li><b>On arrival,</b> switch mobile data to the travel eSIM and turn on data roaming for that line.</li>
    <li><b>Keep your home number</b> on its own line for calls and texts (handy for bank codes), with roaming switched off to avoid charges.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Which phones support eSIM?</h2>
  <p>
    Most phones from about 2019 onwards do: iPhone XS, XR and newer (iPhones sold in the US since the iPhone 14 are eSIM-only),
    Google Pixel 3 and newer, and recent Samsung Galaxy S, Z and many A-series models. Check your phone’s settings for an ‘Add
    eSIM’ option, and confirm with your carrier that it’s unlocked.
  </p>

  <h2>How much data do you need?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Light use (maps, messages, email):</b> around 1 GB a week.</li>
    <li><b>Typical use (plus social media and photos):</b> 3–5 GB a week.</li>
    <li><b>Heavy use (video calls, streaming, hotspot for a laptop):</b> 10 GB+ a week, or an unlimited plan.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Download offline maps and playlists on hotel Wi-Fi to stretch your allowance.</p>

  <h2>Things to watch for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data-only:</b> most travel eSIMs have no local phone number — use WhatsApp or FaceTime for calls.</li>
    <li><b>Validity and speed:</b> check how long the plan lasts and whether speeds are throttled after a fair-use limit.</li>
    <li><b>Hotspot:</b> not every plan allows tethering.</li>
    <li><b>Multi-country trips:</b> a regional plan (Europe, Southeast Asia, Latin America) is usually simpler than several country plans.</li>
    <li><b>China:</b> many travel eSIMs route data abroad, which means apps blocked in mainland China, like Google and WhatsApp, may still work — check the plan details.</li>
  </ul>
  {
    links.length > 0 && (
      <div class="card partner">
        <h2 class="h3">Get a travel eSIM</h2>
        {links.map((l) => (
          <a class="btn" href={l.url} rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank">
            <Icon name={l.kind} size={18} /> {l.cta}
          </a>
        ))}
        <p class="disclosure">Affiliate link — we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">How affiliate links work</a></p>
      </div>
    )
  }
  <p>See also: <a href="/guides/travel-insurance/">travel insurance explained</a>.</p>
</Guide>

